{"id":"/docs/integrations/overview-6","page_id":"/docs/integrations/overview","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/integrations/overview#next-steps","content":"Next steps"},"673":{"id":"/docs/integrations/overview-7","page_id":"/docs/integrations/overview","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/overview","content":"Gent ships with a full library of integrations built on the &#x2A;*Model Context Protocol (MCP)**. These connectors let your AI agents interact with external services — GitHub, Slack, databases, cloud providers, and more — directly from agent chat."},"674":{"id":"/docs/integrations/overview-8","page_id":"/docs/integrations/overview","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/overview#what-is-mcp","content":"The Model Context Protocol is an open standard for connecting AI assistants to external tools. Each MCP connector runs as a local process that speaks a standardized protocol over stdin/stdout. The connector exposes \"tools\" that the AI agent can call — like creating a GitHub issue, querying a database, or sending a Slack message."},"675":{"id":"/docs/integrations/overview-9","page_id":"/docs/integrations/overview","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/overview#how-connectors-work-in-gent","content":"When you enable a connector in Gent:"},"676":{"id":"/docs/integrations/overview-10","page_id":"/docs/integrations/overview","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/overview#how-connectors-work-in-gent","content":"Gent stores your credentials locally (API keys, tokens) in encrypted storage on your device"},"677":{"id":"/docs/integrations/overview-11","page_id":"/docs/integrations/overview","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/overview#how-connectors-work-in-gent","content":"When you start an agent chat, Gent spawns the connector process and passes it to the CLI as an MCP server"},"678":{"id":"/docs/integrations/overview-12","page_id":"/docs/integrations/overview","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/overview#how-connectors-work-in-gent","content":"The AI agent discovers the connector's tools and can call them during the conversation"},"679":{"id":"/docs/integrations/overview-13","page_id":"/docs/integrations/overview","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/overview#how-connectors-work-in-gent","content":"The connector process runs locally on your machine — credentials never leave your device"},"680":{"id":"/docs/integrations/overview-14","page_id":"/docs/integrations/overview","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/overview#how-connectors-work-in-gent","content":"Gent bundles its own Node.js runtime, so most connectors work out of the box without you needing to install Node.js separately. The bundled runtime handles `npx` commands that connectors use to start."},"681":{"id":"/docs/integrations/overview-15","page_id":"/docs/integrations/overview","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/overview#first-party-agent-surfaces","content":"Some connectors are built by Gent itself and are always close to the chat workflow:"},"682":{"id":"/docs/integrations/overview-16","page_id":"/docs/integrations/overview","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/overview#first-party-agent-surfaces","content":"Connector"},"683":{"id":"/docs/integrations/overview-17","page_id":"/docs/integrations/overview","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/overview#first-party-agent-surfaces","content":"What it gives agents"},"684":{"id":"/docs/integrations/overview-18","page_id":"/docs/integrations/overview","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/overview#first-party-agent-surfaces","content":"Gent Canvas"},"685":{"id":"/docs/integrations/overview-19","page_id":"/docs/integrations/overview","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/integrations/overview#first-party-agent-surfaces","content":"A sandboxed HTML/JS/CSS renderer beside the chat for web artifacts, charts, demos, and prototypes"},"686":{"id":"/docs/integrations/overvie